# --- Go binaries (this project) ---
# Anchored to their real build locations. NEVER use a bare `ouija` pattern:
# `cmd/ouija/` is a source directory and a bare pattern would ignore it.
/ouija
/ouija-mcp
/cmd/ouija/ouija
/cmd/ouija-mcp/ouija-mcp

# --- release artifacts (make dist) ---
/dist/

# Compiled objects / shared libs (cross-platform safety)
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# --- Go test / coverage / profiling ---
*.test
*.out
*.prof
coverage.*

# --- Go workspace (local multi-module dev) ---
go.work
go.work.sum

# --- ouija runtime output ---
# (comments MUST be on their own line — a trailing "# ..." becomes part of the pattern)
*.jsonl
.ouija/
.ouija.json
# Project config discovered at the repo root when it walks up; may carry an
# apiKey override, so it is ignored for the same reason as .ouija/.
ouija.json

# --- generated docs (written by totems/commands, not hand-written) ---
# `ouija doc` writes <slug>.html/.pdf/.md and `ouija learn` writes docs/knowledge.md.
# Here we only ignore the machine-rendered binaries; see the note at the bottom
# about what is intentionally kept.
docs/*.html
docs/*.pdf
# `ouija task` rewrites its plan on every run.
docs/task-plan.md

# Temp files of an atomic write interrupted by a crash (internal/atomicfile).
.*.tmp-*

# --- secrets / local env ---
.env
.env.*
.envrc
*.pem
*.key

# --- Claude Code (never commit local assistant state) ---
.claude/

# --- dumps, archives, backups, logs ---
*.sql
*.sql.gz
*.dump
*.bak
*.orig
*.rej
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
*.zip
*.log

# --- editor / IDE ---
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# --- OS junk ---
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# --- intentionally NOT ignored ---
# Hand-written documentation (README, docs/**/*.md — including the style guide
# and docs/explanation/architecture.md), LICENSE, CONTRIBUTING.md, SECURITY.md,
# CHANGELOG.md, go.mod/go.sum, Makefile, release-please config, assets/*.svg,
# totems/**/template.* and testdata.
# docs/knowledge.md (ouija learn) and docs/sticker/*.sticker.svg (sticker totem)
# are generated but kept on purpose: they are shareable project knowledge/art.
# Trace files are named by you (`--trace-file <path>`), so if you keep one in the
# repo add its exact name or pattern here.
